  "answer": "The study confirmed only 1 of the 5 cases it set out to find. That case is scene 096988dd with the lead car shifted 1.5 s later at half speed (S8). Even there, the policy never rear-ended the lead car. Every failure the videos explain is the ego drifting left out of its lane, against a RIGHT or STRAIGHT route command. It then hit a vehicle travelling alongside in the left lane, or left the road once. So the answer is no: within what was tested, no retiming of the lead car made VaVAM run into it. The confirmed case is a lane-keeping failure that shows up when the lead car comes later and slower. A 2.0 s shift on the same scene (S9) also failed every time, but two runs are a hint, not a rate. On 12855a41, failures at 1.5 s and 2.0 s shifts were occasional, and their ranges still allow anything from rare to frequent. Almost every setting tried was at half speed with a delayed lead car. Earlier timings and faster lead cars are largely untested.",
